{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 retrieved models, only three are direct trait matches by trait_reported/trait_efo (PGS005252, PGS005254) or are disease-adjacent but still use an incident aortic stenosis endpoint (PGS005255/PGS005256). Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy scores (PGS000739/PGS000778/PGS004910/PGS004911) are not direct matches to the target trait (aortic stenosis) and are not considered further.

Within the aortic-stenosis–aligned set, PGS005254 is preferred because it targets aortic stenosis with incident-case phenotyping and uses only basic demographic/ancestry covariates (sex, age, age^2, PCs 1–5), and it reports a per-SD hazard ratio with CI (HR=1.64, 95% CI 1.50–1.78), providing PRS-specific signal without relying on a covariate-inflated full-model discrimination metric.

The main limitation is that PGS005254 lacks comparable discrimination metrics (no AUROC/C-index/R² reported), while PGS005252 reports a high C-index (0.87) but with many clinical comorbidity covariates (e.g., diabetes, hypertension, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure) that domain guidance flags as potentially inflating full-model performance for cardiovascular outcomes, making that C-index less informative for PRS quality ranking. Given these metric-comparability gaps, the recommendation is direct-match but sub-optimal in certainty."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005265","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among 7 direct-match candidates, three are exact Graves’ disease endpoints (PGS005264/PGS005265/PGS005266) and four are broader hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis endpoints (PGS001042/PGS001043/PGS001815/PGS002023). Per the provided policy, endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported) is the top criterion, so the Graves’ disease models are preferred over thyrotoxicosis/hyperthyroidism proxies despite those proxy models having cleaner PGS-only metrics reported in UKB.

Within the Graves’ disease set, PGS005265 is the best-supported based on the visible evidence: it has the highest reported full-model AUROC (0.6652 vs 0.6637 and 0.6587), is genome-wide (≈1.09M variants) using PRSCS, and reports a stronger per-SD effect size (OR 1.625) than the other Graves’ disease candidates (OR 1.543 and 1.008). All three share the same publication family, validation size (n=94,651), and have unknown covariates, so the small AUROC/OR advantages are the main differentiators.

The recommendation remains sub-optimal because key comparability evidence is missing/unclear for the Graves’ disease models (covariates are unknown; only full-model AUROC is shown and no PGS-only AUROC/R² is provided), and the best-performing proxy models (thyrotoxicosis/hyperthyroidism) provide stronger PRS-only metric reporting but are not phenotype-identical to Graves’ disease."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004935","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All listed candidates are direct matches at the trait/efo level (hypothyroidism), but they vary materially in endpoint fidelity and comparability of reported performance. Several early scores (PGS000759/PGS000761/PGS000820) are for immune-checkpoint-inhibitor–induced hypothyroidism, a therapy-induced endpoint that is explicitly down-ranked versus generic disease risk. Pan-trait/portability-framework models (e.g., PGS000965/PGS001181 snpnet across 813 traits; PGS001816/PGS002024 portability of 245 scores; PGS002336+ family Nat Genet cross-pop training) are also down-ranked when a disease-focused alternative exists.

Among the remaining generic-endpoint candidates, PGS005218 (Nat Genet 2025) is disease-focused, very large, and reports high full-model AUROC=0.859; however its covariates include thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO) which the provided domain policy flags as near-outcome covariates that severely inflate AUROC and make it non-comparable for PRS quality ranking without PRS-only/incremental metrics. In contrast, PGS004935 has a clean hypothyroidism phenotype, reports an AUROC=0.70 alongside an OR=1.5 with a straightforward UKB-based development context; although covariates are listed as unknown (a limitation), it avoids the explicit biomarker-inflated performance issue and provides a directly comparable discrimination metric.

Confidence is Moderate (not High) because multiple strong disease-focused candidates exist but key comparability fields are missing/unclear for several (notably covariates for PGS004935; and PRS-only/incremental metrics for the biomarker-adjusted PGS005218), leaving residual uncertainty about the best standalone PRS for generic hypothyroidism risk."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates exist for hypothyroidism, but the strongest-looking discrimination report (PGS005218 AUROC=0.859) is from an incident hypothyroidism model whose covariates include thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), which the provided domain guidance flags as near-outcome variables that can severely inflate full-model AUROC and make it non-comparable for PRS quality ranking. Despite this limitation, PGS005218 remains the best-supported direct-match overall because it is from a dedicated hypothyroidism GWAS/PRS study (Nat Genet, 2025-11-10) with very large training (n=1,146,562 across multiple cohorts) and large validation (n=441,692), providing the broadest coherent evidence package among the candidates. Competing models with cleaner covariates either target therapy-induced hypothyroidism (PGS000759/PGS000761/PGS000820) rather than general disease, come from pan-trait or portability frameworks (e.g., snpnet UKB 813-traits: PGS000965/PGS001181; UKB portability: PGS001816/PGS002024) or report weaker/less comparable metrics (e.g., partial-r only, small incremental R2). The main unresolved limitation is the lack of PRS-only or incremental metrics for PGS005218 to isolate PRS contribution from biomarker-driven prediction."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000053","confidence":"Low","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches on concept alignment (trait_efo = late-onset Alzheimer's disease). Endpoint fidelity varies: PGS000053/PGS000054 and PGS000334/PGS004918 all report a direct late-onset AD endpoint (or familial late-onset AD, which domain policy treats as still a direct match for late-onset neurodegenerative disease). PGS002289 is concept-labeled as late-onset AD but its phenotyping_reported appears to be a cognition-by-age interaction phenotype rather than an AD diagnosis endpoint, lowering endpoint fidelity for the target trait.

Among the remaining endpoint-faithful candidates, performance evidence is limited and not directly comparable: PGS000053 provides an OR per SD (1.29, EUR eval n=4,792) with basic covariates (age, sex), but no AUC/R²; PGS000054 shows a stronger OR (1.73) but is evaluated in Hispanic/Latin American ancestry (AMR) rather than EUR, making it less generally supported in this context without a specified deployment ancestry. PGS000334 reports R²=0.191 from a C+T score with unknown covariates, which is unusually high relative to the provided heritability sanity-check context and therefore may be non-comparable/inflated; PGS004918 reports full-model AUROC=0.731 but covariates are unknown and the validation sample is very small (n=136), and domain guidance cautions against promoting candidates based on full-model AUROC alone.

Given these constraints, PGS000053 is the most defensible ‘best’ choice from visible evidence because it has a clear direct endpoint (familial late-onset AD), basic covariate adjustment (age/sex), European evaluation ancestry, and a moderate external validation size relative to others. Confidence remains low because no candidate provides clearly PRS-comparable discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUC/R²) and several competitors have either stronger effect size in a different ancestry (PGS000054) or potentially non-comparable/inflated metrics with unknown covariates (PGS000334, PGS004918)."}
